    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nFor projection from an elevated cliff of height $h$, maximum range is obtained by optimizing the launch angle $\\theta$. The maximum range on the ground is given by $R_{max} = \\frac{u}{g}\\sqrt{u^2 + 2gh}$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nLaunch height $h$, speed $u$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nR_{max} = \\frac{u}{g}\\sqrt{u^2 + 2gh}\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Trajectory equation with ground at $y = -h$:\n   $-h = x\\tan\\theta - \\frac{g x^2}{2u^2}(1 + \\tan^2\\theta)$.\n2. Rearrange as a quadratic in $\\tan\\theta$:\n   $\\frac{gx^2}{2u^2}\\tan^2\\theta - x\\tan\\theta + \\left(\\frac{gx^2}{2u^2} - h\\right) = 0$.\n3. For real solutions of $\\tan\\theta$, discriminant $\\Delta \\ge 0$:\n   $x^2 - 4 \\left(\\frac{gx^2}{2u^2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{gx^2}{2u^2} - h\\right) \\ge 0$.\n4. Divide by $x^2$:\n   $1 - \\frac{2g}{u^2} \\left(\\frac{gx^2}{2u^2} - h\\right) \\ge 0 \\implies 1 + \\frac{2gh}{u^2} \\ge \\frac{g^2 x^2}{u^4}$.\n5. Take square root:\n   $x_{max} = \\frac{u^2}{g} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{2gh}{u^2}} = \\frac{u}{g}\\sqrt{u^2 + 2gh}$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: $\\frac{u}{g}\\sqrt{u^2 + 2gh}$\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nNotice that if $h = 0$, $R_{max} = \\frac{u}{g}\\sqrt{u^2} = \\frac{u^2}{g}$, which matches standard ground-to-ground maximum range!",
